      "text": "Let $AE = CF = x$.  Let $x_1$ be the height of $A$ when the square is folded along $DE$.  Since $A$ has to land on $BD$ after the square is folded along $DE$, we have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{x_1^2-x^2} + 1&=x_1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(1 - x)^2 - x^2} + 1 &= ?!!\n\\end{align*} \n$$\n\nwhere the problem specifies that $F$ is supposed to be the point where the square touches $BC$ after the square is folded along $DF$.  To solve for $x$, we first need to recalculate $k$ using the fact that the length of $DE$ minus $DF$ is $1$, which we can formulate as:\n\n$$\\sqrt{x_1^2-x^2}+\\sqrt{(1-x)^2-x^2} =1$$\n\nSquaring both sides of that last equation, we obtain:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nx^2+x(1-x)+x_1^2-x^2 &=\\left(\\sqrt{x_1^2-x^2}+\\sqrt{(1-x)^2-x^2}\\right)^2\\\\\n&=x_1^2+x^2(1-x)+2\\sqrt{(x_1-x)(x_1+(1-x))}.\n\\end{align*}\n   \\begin{align*}\n0&=x^2-x_1^2+x^2(1-x)+2\\sqrt{x_1(1-x)(1+x)}\\\\\n&=1-x_1^2+x^2-x^2+2\\sqrt{x_1(1-x)(1+x)}\\\\\n&=1+2\\sqrt{x_1(1-x)(1+x)}\\\\\n&=x_1(1+x).\n\\end{align*}\n   \nWe can now factor out a $1-x_1$ (the left side of the above equation when the side length is a \n1 )\n\n\\begin{align*}\nx_1(1+x)-(1+x) &=0\\\\\n&(x_1-1)(x+1) &=0\\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nThis gives us $x_1=-1$ and $x=-1$.",
